[
    {
        "content": "<p>Let <code>l</code>, <code>m</code>, <code>n</code> be lists. Say <code>n</code> is <code>l</code> and <code>m</code> merged together if <code>n</code> can be partitioned into sublists <code>l</code> and <code>m</code> while maintaining the order.<br>\nIs there API for this predicate?</p>",
        "id": 623221355,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1789062470
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't think we have this, and <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"387244\">@Yaël Dillies</span> wants to take the name <code>List.Interleaves</code> for strict alternation</p>",
        "id": 623222598,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1789062772
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No, not just strict. <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39870\">#39870</a> introduces <code>List.Interleaves</code>for an arbitrary relation, which can be strict or not. I don't see how this is related to what Artie is asking for however, because they don't seem to care about the lists being exactly interleaved: <code>n := l ++ m</code> would satisfy Artie's predicate, right?</p>",
        "id": 623227515,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1789063776
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes that's right<br>\nLLM generated description of what I mean:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">merges</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[],</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">l₂</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₁</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₁</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₁</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">''</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">merges</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₂</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∪</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">''</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">merges</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₁</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₂</span>\n<span class=\"n\">termination_by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₁</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l₂</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">length</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 623227759,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1789063827
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Two lists <code>l</code> and <code>m</code> merge into <code>n</code> iff there are lists <code>el</code> and <code>em</code> and <code>en</code> such that <code>l = el.flatten</code>, <code>m = em.flatten</code>, <code>n = en.flatten</code>, and <code>en</code> is the interleaving of <code>el</code> and <code>em</code>?</p>",
        "id": 623228884,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1789064098
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't see what flatten has to do with it</p>",
        "id": 623229601,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1789064195
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"400289\">Artie Khovanov</span> Aaron is just rephrasing it by partitioning each parent list into a list of sublists that appear verbatim (uninterrupted) in the merged string</p>",
        "id": 623300696,
        "sender_full_name": "Matt Diamond",
        "timestamp": 1789086331
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>so from that perspective, <code>flatten</code> undoes the partitioning and gets you back the original lists</p>",
        "id": 623300917,
        "sender_full_name": "Matt Diamond",
        "timestamp": 1789086463
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816344\">Aaron Liu</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/Set.20of.20merges.20of.20lists/near/623228884\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Two lists <code>l</code> and <code>m</code> merge into <code>n</code> iff there are lists <code>el</code> and <code>em</code> and <code>en</code> such that <code>l = el.flatten</code>, <code>m = em.flatten</code>, <code>n = en.flatten</code>, and <code>en</code> is the interleaving of <code>el</code> and <code>em</code>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>phrasing it this way I can see this is just the pushforward of the interleaving relation along <code>List.flatten</code></p>",
        "id": 623302136,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1789087015
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think Artie's predicate is the usual <strong>shuffle</strong> relation on words/lists, rather than <code>Interleaves</code> in the alternating sense.</p>\n<p>Would a direct inductive relation be a more convenient API than going through partitions/<code>flatten</code>?</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsShuffle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">nil</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsShuffle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">left</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">IsShuffle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">IsShuffle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">right</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">IsShuffle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">IsShuffle</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Then Artie's set is just</p>\n<p><code>{n | List.IsShuffle l m n}</code></p>\n<p>and <code>l ++ m</code> is immediately a shuffle of <code>l</code> and <code>m</code>.</p>\n<p>Aaron's description seems like an equivalent characterization by chunking the two inputs and strictly interleaving the chunks, but the direct inductive relation might give a nicer induction principle/API. It also makes clear that arbitrary runs from either input are allowed.</p>\n<p>If something like this doesn't already exist, perhaps <code>List.IsShuffle</code> / <code>List.Shuffles</code> would avoid the naming collision with <code>List.Interleaves</code>.</p>",
        "id": 623684100,
        "sender_full_name": "Zeping Cheng",
        "timestamp": 1789177307
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Shuffle is the wrong word IMO but yes an inductive predicate is probably a better formalisation</p>",
        "id": 623687876,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1789179342
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>BTW my original use case is no longer relevant</p>",
        "id": 623687891,
        "sender_full_name": "Artie Khovanov",
        "timestamp": 1789179356
    }
]